From fbdebfdd27d5b4e61ecb052af9d3876605c37ee0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Changpeng Liu <changpeliu@tencent.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 17:31:29 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] bdev/aio: flush with fdatasync rather than fsync

A bdev flush has to make the written blocks readable after a crash, and
nothing more. fdatasync covers exactly that -- including the block
allocation and the unwritten-extent conversion needed to read the data
back -- while fsync additionally commits the inode timestamps, which are
not part of the bdev abstraction at all: a bdev's size never changes
after it is created, and no caller can observe an aio bdev's mtime
through the bdev API.

The cost of the difference is not marginal. Measured on ext4 over a
write-through virtio disk, 4 KiB O_DIRECT writes to already-written
extents:

    write only            647 us
    write + fdatasync     674 us
    write + fsync        2177 us

The 1.5 ms is a jbd2 commit for the timestamp update. It is paid on every
flush, so a caller that treats write+flush as one commit unit -- a
write-ahead log, typically -- pays it per commit. Worse, FLUSH is served
by a synchronous syscall here rather than through the aio ring, so it is
paid by blocking the calling reactor: an SPDK poller sits in the kernel
for the duration.

For a WAL on an aio bdev this is the whole difference between roughly 440
and 1480 commits per second, without giving up any durability the caller
had.

Signed-off-by: Changpeng Liu <changpeliu@tencent.com>
Assisted-by: CodeBuddy
---
 module/bdev/aio/bdev_aio.c | 20 +++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/module/bdev/aio/bdev_aio.c b/module/bdev/aio/bdev_aio.c
index 23a0dc609..5680ead75 100644
--- a/module/bdev/aio/bdev_aio.c
+++ b/module/bdev/aio/bdev_aio.c
@@ -308,7 +308,25 @@ bdev_aio_rw(enum spdk_bdev_io_type type, struct file_disk *fdisk,
 static void
 bdev_aio_flush(struct file_disk *fdisk, struct bdev_aio_task *aio_task)
 {
-	int rc = fsync(fdisk->fd);
+	/* fdatasync, not fsync: a bdev flush has to make the written blocks
+	 * readable after a crash, and nothing more. fdatasync covers exactly
+	 * that, including the block allocation and unwritten-extent conversion
+	 * needed to read the data back. What it leaves out -- the inode
+	 * timestamps -- is not part of the bdev abstraction: a bdev's size never
+	 * changes after it is created, and no caller can observe an aio bdev's
+	 * mtime through the bdev API.
+	 *
+	 * The difference is not small. Measured on ext4 over a write-through
+	 * virtio disk, 4 KiB O_DIRECT writes to already-written extents: 674 us
+	 * per write with fdatasync against 2177 us with fsync, where the write
+	 * alone is 647 us. The 1.5 ms is a jbd2 commit for the timestamp update,
+	 * and it is paid on every flush. Callers that treat write+flush as one
+	 * commit unit -- a write-ahead log, typically -- pay it per commit, and
+	 * because SPDK_BDEV_IO_TYPE_FLUSH is served by a synchronous syscall
+	 * here rather than through the aio ring, it is paid by blocking the
+	 * calling reactor.
+	 */
+	int rc = fdatasync(fdisk->fd);
 
 	if (rc == 0) {
 		spdk_bdev_io_complete(spdk_bdev_io_from_ctx(aio_task), SPDK_BDEV_IO_STATUS_SUCCESS);
-- 
